# A single press is a single click, even where two lines meet.
#
# Transcript lines are stacked with no gap, so line N's bottom edge is line
# N+1's top edge. The hit test used to be inclusive on both, so a press on that
# seam was inside BOTH elements, the multi-click run — a global, counted per
# element hit rather than per press — incremented twice, and a single click
# selected a WORD. Not a test artifact: it is what a person gets for clicking
# between two lines of a transcript, and it is invisible unless you happen to
# click on the pixel.
#
# y=260 is that seam in this thread at 1100x760: the first account line's
# element is (329,244 656x16) and the second's is (329,260 656x16).
# HANABI_SELECT_AUDIT=1 prints both, which is how it was found:
#
#   [sel] press=(415.0,260.0) run=1 rect=(329.0,244.0 656.0x16.0) len=61
#   [sel] press=(415.0,260.0) run=2 rect=(329.0,260.0 656.0x16.0) len=58
#
# It was 234 (over lines at 218 and 234) until the six feature branches of
# 2026-08-26 landed and moved every body line down 26px -- this script and
# select_word_and_line.e2e were RED on main at 88fde14 for that reason and
# that reason alone, with the selection code itself untouched and correct.
# Re-measured with the audit knob rather than nudged; the current reading is
# quoted above so the next move is a diff and not an investigation.
#
# The assertion is the composer's readout, the same observable
# select_word_and_line.e2e uses: after one click there is nothing selected.
#
# env: HANABI_SELECT_AUDIT=1
#
# The fixture's clock is pinned by the runner, for the reason
# select_word_and_line.e2e sets out in full: a "Today" date divider appears and
# disappears with the wall clock and moves every coordinate below it by its
# own 26px.
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}

require_thread t2

expect_no_text selected

click 415 234
wait_frames 20
expect_no_text selected

# And a real double click on the same seam still takes a word, so the fix is
# "count presses", not "ignore the seam".
#
# The click away first is not decoration. The multi-click window is 400ms of
# WALL CLOCK and a scripted run's waits are frame counts (afterhours_gaps.md
# #231), so a whole script runs inside one 400ms window and every press at the
# same point continues the same run -- without this, the double below would be
# presses three and four of the run started above and would take the LINE
# (58 selected) rather than the word (6).
#
# IT HAS TO LAND ON AN ELEMENT, and the reason is the whole subject of this
# script: the run is counted per ELEMENT HIT, so a press that hits nothing
# hits no element, the counter is never touched, and the run survives however
# far away it was. Measured: with a press into empty transcript the audit
# reads runs 1, 2, 3 at the seam and the double below is a TRIPLE. Waiting
# longer does not help either -- `wait_frames 60` fails identically, which is
# #231 from the other side: a headless run's frames are stepped, so a frame
# count cannot spend the 400ms of wall clock the window is measured in.
#
# 700,300 IS on an element with the clock pinned: the reply's body element is
# (329,289 656x32), so this is a real hit 285px along it, far enough in x to
# reset the run. It was replaced by a coordinate on another line while the
# date divider was pushing everything 26px down and this press fell off the
# bottom of the turn -- the same drift the header describes, from the other
# end.
click 700 300
wait_frames 6

double_click 415 234
wait_frames 20
expect_text "6 selected"
